1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:06,784
Impedance is why almost every connector, cable, and radio you will ever plug

2
00:00:06,784 --> 00:00:10,246
together has the same number printed on it: fifty ohms.

3
00:00:10,246 --> 00:00:15,344
It's the total opposition a circuit, a component, or a transmission line presents

4
00:00:15,344 --> 00:00:20,254
to an alternating current — and radio standardizes on one shared value so that

5
00:00:20,254 --> 00:00:23,842
power flows from source to antenna without bouncing back.

6
00:00:24,292 --> 00:00:28,709
Written out, impedance is a complex number: Z equals R plus j X.

7
00:00:28,709 --> 00:00:30,849
The R part is plain resistance.

8
00:00:30,849 --> 00:00:36,577
The X part is reactance — the opposition from capacitors and inductors, which store

9
00:00:36,577 --> 00:00:41,685
energy and hand it back, shifting the current's phase against the voltage.

10
00:00:41,685 --> 00:00:47,344
One number, two personalities: how much the signal is held back, and how much it's

11
00:00:47,344 --> 00:00:48,448
twisted in time.

12
00:00:48,898 --> 00:00:50,712
Here's the everyday picture.

13
00:00:50,712 --> 00:00:54,338
Tie a thin rope to a thick one and flick a wave down it.

14
00:00:54,338 --> 00:00:59,714
At the knot, part of the wave keeps going — and part reflects straight back at you.

15
00:00:59,714 --> 00:01:01,980
That knot is an impedance mismatch.

16
00:01:01,980 --> 00:01:07,031
A radio signal traveling down a cable does exactly the same thing anywhere the

17
00:01:07,031 --> 00:01:11,629
impedance changes: some power continues forward, and some bounces home.

18
00:01:12,079 --> 00:01:17,179
A cable like coax has a characteristic impedance — fifty ohms for most RF coax —

19
00:01:17,179 --> 00:01:20,239
fixed purely by its geometry and its insulation.

20
00:01:20,239 --> 00:01:24,893
It's the ratio of voltage to current for a wave traveling along the line.

21
00:01:24,893 --> 00:01:29,802
And here's the trap: it is not a resistance you can measure with an ohmmeter.

22
00:01:29,802 --> 00:01:34,328
Put a meter across a disconnected coax and you'll read an open circuit.

23
00:01:34,328 --> 00:01:38,918
Fifty ohms is what the cable looks like to a signal propagating down it.

24
00:01:39,368 --> 00:01:44,362
Maximum power transfers — and nothing reflects — only when the impedances agree.

25
00:01:44,362 --> 00:01:49,543
Match a fifty-ohm source through fifty-ohm cable into a fifty-ohm load and the wave

26
00:01:49,543 --> 00:01:50,916
flows through cleanly.

27
00:01:50,916 --> 00:01:56,097
Mismatch the far end and part of the wave comes back, piling up against the forward

28
00:01:56,097 --> 00:01:58,594
wave into standing waves along the line.

29
00:01:58,594 --> 00:02:02,526
Every watt in that reflection is a watt that never did its job.

30
00:02:02,976 --> 00:02:04,979
So why fifty, of all numbers?

31
00:02:04,979 --> 00:02:06,844
Pure historical compromise.

32
00:02:06,844 --> 00:02:12,576
Coaxial cable handles peak power best near thirty ohms and has its lowest loss near

33
00:02:12,576 --> 00:02:13,543
seventy-seven.

34
00:02:13,543 --> 00:02:19,068
Fifty sits usefully between the two, with convenient physical dimensions — so it

35
00:02:19,068 --> 00:02:20,656
became the RF standard.

36
00:02:20,656 --> 00:02:26,319
Video and broadcast gear went the other way and standardized on seventy-five ohms,

37
00:02:26,319 --> 00:02:30,394
which is why TV coax and radio coax are not the same cable.

38
00:02:30,844 --> 00:02:36,241
In the real world, an antenna's feedpoint rarely lands exactly on fifty ohms across

39
00:02:36,241 --> 00:02:41,378
a whole band, so matching networks — series and shunt inductors and capacitors,

40
00:02:41,378 --> 00:02:46,775
tuners, transformers — cancel the leftover reactance and rotate the resistance back

41
00:02:46,775 --> 00:02:47,945
toward the target.

42
00:02:47,945 --> 00:02:53,212
On receive, a bad mismatch mostly costs signal rather than hardware: part of what

43
00:02:53,212 --> 00:02:57,829
the antenna caught reflects away instead of ever reaching the receiver.

44
00:02:58,279 --> 00:03:03,451
Every SDR front end GopherTrunk listens through presents a nominal fifty-ohm input,

45
00:03:03,451 --> 00:03:08,622
and the amplifier, filters, and mixer behind that connector are all designed around

46
00:03:08,622 --> 00:03:08,809
it.

47
00:03:08,809 --> 00:03:13,856
GopherTrunk itself works on the digital samples after the front end, so it cannot

48
00:03:13,856 --> 00:03:18,903
fix a mismatch in software — a control channel that arrives weak because half the

49
00:03:18,903 --> 00:03:24,075
signal reflected off a bad match stays weak on the dBFS meter, and no amount of DSP

50
00:03:24,075 --> 00:03:24,823
recovers it.

51
00:03:24,823 --> 00:03:29,620
A resonant, reasonably matched antenna beats a random length of wire, and the

52
00:03:29,620 --> 00:03:32,923
signal-to-noise readout will show you the difference.

53
00:03:33,373 --> 00:03:38,322
So: impedance is R plus j X — opposition with a phase twist; fifty ohms is the

54
00:03:38,322 --> 00:03:43,525
shared reference that keeps power moving forward instead of reflecting; and a good

55
00:03:43,525 --> 00:03:47,840
match at the antenna is worth more than any setting in the software.

56
00:03:47,840 --> 00:03:49,553
Full write-up linked below.

